WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.620
Everything in this video was made by

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:03.720
AI agents.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.570
The script,

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.480
the voice,

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:06.330
the animation,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.410
even the music.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:08.900
But none of it was left to

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:09.750
them alone.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:10.460
Rasmus,

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.820
who wrote the book this channel is

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:12.670
based on,

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.940
steered every step.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.950
That's what engineering looks like now.

00:00:20.162 --> 00:00:21.192
For 20 years,

00:00:21.242 --> 00:00:22.632
the job was one loop.

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:23.222
You,

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:24.122
a keyboard,

00:00:24.122 --> 00:00:25.052
and a problem.

00:00:25.182 --> 00:00:26.692
That loop is changing.

00:00:26.942 --> 00:00:29.012
Coding agents read whole code bases,

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.532
edit dozens of files,

00:00:30.622 --> 00:00:31.502
run the tests,

00:00:31.502 --> 00:00:32.792
and fix what fails.

00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:34.462
More of the work is now describing

00:00:34.462 --> 00:00:35.322
what you want,

00:00:35.322 --> 00:00:36.762
reviewing what comes back,

00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:37.632
and steering.

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:39.397
Here's the catch.

00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:41.547
Working well with agents is a skill.

00:00:41.637 --> 00:00:43.787
When an agent wastes your afternoon,

00:00:43.837 --> 00:00:45.397
it's rarely a weak model.

00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:46.877
It's missing context,

00:00:46.877 --> 00:00:48.677
a task scoped too wide,

00:00:48.677 --> 00:00:50.477
or not knowing when to let it

00:00:50.477 --> 00:00:50.797
run,

00:00:50.797 --> 00:00:52.537
and when to take the wheel back.

00:00:52.537 --> 00:00:53.857
None of that is complicated.

00:00:53.857 --> 00:00:55.467
It just isn't obvious.

00:00:56.358 --> 00:00:58.678
And nobody has this fully figured out.

00:00:58.678 --> 00:01:00.828
The field moves every month.

00:01:00.918 --> 00:01:03.418
What this series offers is principles that

00:01:03.418 --> 00:01:05.198
have held up in real projects,

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:06.378
stated plainly,

00:01:06.378 --> 00:01:07.828
failures included.

00:01:07.898 --> 00:01:08.858
No hype.

00:01:08.858 --> 00:01:09.828
No doom.

00:01:09.958 --> 00:01:11.188
No prompt tricks.

00:01:12.041 --> 00:01:13.761
The Agentic Crew is a free book

00:01:13.761 --> 00:01:15.171
by Rasmus Schlünsen.

00:01:15.301 --> 00:01:17.501
This channel turns each chapter into a

00:01:17.501 --> 00:01:19.251
short film like this one.

00:01:19.481 --> 00:01:20.161
Context,

00:01:20.161 --> 00:01:21.231
guardrails,

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:21.741
git,

00:01:21.741 --> 00:01:22.541
tests,

00:01:22.541 --> 00:01:23.201
memory,

00:01:23.201 --> 00:01:24.051
security,

00:01:24.081 --> 00:01:25.091
fleets of agents,

00:01:25.101 --> 00:01:26.741
and knowing when not to use them.

00:01:26.741 --> 00:01:28.231
About two minutes each.

00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:29.832
Next up,

00:01:29.832 --> 00:01:31.472
what an agent actually is.

00:01:31.472 --> 00:01:32.632
Here's a hint.

00:01:32.632 --> 00:01:35.262
The model never runs a single command.

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:37.432
Subscribe so you don't miss it and

00:01:37.432 --> 00:01:39.712
read the whole book free at theagenticcrew

00:01:39.712 --> 00:01:40.602
.com.
